<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Eras are SimWorld's translation of RimWorld's TechLevel ladder into "civilization through the ages": a
    civilization-wide age that completes once every research project tagged to it (ResearchProjectDef.era)
    is finished. order is the ladder position (0 = earliest); techLevel is the TechLevel band the era
    occupies for CostFactor and, later, faction/settlement generation. Exotic's techLevel is Archotech, the
    top of RimWorld's ladder, standing in for both "Ultra" and "Archotech" until SimWorld's own translation
    of that end of the ladder is designed.

    An era does not multiply threat points. A threatPointsFactor ladder (1.0 to 3.4) did until 2026-11; it
    bought raiders of unchanged strength and nothing else, so it was removed. See
    StorytellerUtility.DefaultThreatPointsNow for the evidence, and docs/research/era-arc.md finding 5 for
    how era pressure is meant to return (as strength, not head count).
  -->

  <!--
    Era progression (docs/design/era-progression.md; docs/status.json research translation).

    THE MODEL. The civilization is IN an era while it learns that era's research and may issue its edicts
    (ResearchManager.CurrentEra). It leaves for the next era once this era's research spine is finished and every
    one of this era's <conditions> holds (EraDef.GateOpen). So each era's conditions below are the way OUT of it.
    A new game is in the first era; a scenario that starts in an era has finished every era before it. Until
    2026-11-24 the era was the last one complete, so a fresh start was "in" an era it had not learned and an
    Agrarian village start refused its own edicts.

    PACING. intendedYears is the years a nominal settlement of 25 spends IN the era, learning it. A year is 60
    days. Intent: an evening of play (about 300 days at speed 5) crosses four era turns, and the whole ladder is
    learned in about eighteen years, with Exotic lived in from about year 14 and the endless tail behind it.
      SticksAndStones 0.25 (the starting kit and the first roofs), Agrarian 1, Bronze 1.5, Classical 2,
      Medieval 2.5, Industrial 3, Information 3.5, Exotic 4.
    The world enters each era on the same clock (Factions.FactionAdvancement): Agrarian day 15, Bronze 75,
    Classical 165, Medieval 285, Industrial 435, Information 615, Exotic 825.
    The ladder was twice as long (2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 years with the era complete). In a fresh-eyes playtest an
    era turned every 50-70 real minutes and nothing changed when it did, and a ten-year arc ended in Classical or
    Medieval. Halved, the turns come every 25-60 minutes early and each one now says what it changed.

    citizensPerScholar sets the research benches a settlement wants in its current era (one per that many
    citizens, rounded, at least one). Not sourced. The values moved one era later with the model, so the benches
    a settlement has while it learns each era are the ones the pacing was measured with: 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 4
    for a settlement of 25.

    researchCostFactor multiplies every project in the era, on top of RimWorld's tech-level factor (research is
    priced at the tech level of the era mastered, ResearchManager.AdvanceTechLevelToEra). Derived, not tuned per
    seed: the days in era X pay for X's research at the points the settlement earns while in it,
      factor(X) = days(X) x points per day(X) / (authored cost of X x tech factor(X))
    Points per day are measured (docs/research/era-arc.md section 10.3): about 600 with one bench, about 390 a
    bench with two, and 390 a bench assumed beyond. Every factor is the previous derivation's at half the days:
      SticksAndStones               4,500 x 1.0, the starting kit       -> 1.0
      Agrarian                     12,100 x 1.0, 60 d,    600/day -> 3.0
      Bronze                       21,110 x 1.5, 90 d,    600/day -> 1.7
      Classical                    38,060 x 1.0, 120 d,   600/day -> 1.9
      Medieval                     60,930 x 1.0, 150 d,   600/day -> 1.5
      Industrial                  120,210 x 1.5, 180 d,   780/day -> 0.78
      Information                 161,370 x 1.5, 210 d, 1,170/day -> 1.0
      Exotic                      163,680 x 2.0, 240 d, 1,560/day -> 1.15
    Industrial is priced under RimWorld's rate: its authored tree is the largest and the settlement has two
    benches for it. Generated endless projects carry the last era's factor (Research.EraPacing.CostFactorFor).
    EraPacingTests recomputes the nominal days from the loaded content and pins them to the intent.

    CONDITIONS (Research.EraCondition). Each reads state the settlement's own machinery produces, so an unwatched
    town can clear it, and each has a player lever. Not sourced: the bars are this port's, and the later ones are
    provisional until the CI arcs in docs/design/era-progression.md section 6 report how long each era holds.
      SticksAndStones: half the people sleep under their own roof. The first killer was the weather.
      Agrarian:        10 days of food in store. A surplus frees hands from the fields.
      Bronze:          a smithy stands. The age is the smith's.
      Classical:       15 days of food, and three in four people housed.
      Medieval:        24 people. A kingdom needs subjects.
      Industrial:      28 people and 20 days of food.
      Information:     32 people.
  -->

  <EraDef>
    <defName>SticksAndStones</defName>
    <label>sticks and stones</label>
    <description>Fire, stone tools, and the first words spoken around a hearth. The band learns to cook, to build a roof, and to hunt together.</description>
    <order>0</order>
    <techLevel>Neolithic</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>25</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>0.25</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_Housed">
        <share>0.5</share>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Agrarian</defName>
    <label>agrarian</label>
    <description>Farming, storage, and permanent settlement replace the hunt and the chase. Fields are sown, granaries filled, and the first longhouses raised.</description>
    <order>1</order>
    <techLevel>Neolithic</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>3.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>25</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>1</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_FoodStores">
        <days>10</days>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Bronze</defName>
    <label>bronze age</label>
    <description>Smelting and alloying open the door to tools and weapons stone cannot match. The smith's fire becomes the heart of the town.</description>
    <order>2</order>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.7</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>25</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>1.5</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_Building">
        <things>
          <li>Smithy</li>
        </things>
        <label>a smithy</label>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Classical</defName>
    <label>classical</label>
    <description>Law, mathematics, and monumental building organize a civilization at scale. Scholars are kept, and the town is planned in streets.</description>
    <order>3</order>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.9</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>20</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>2</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_FoodStores">
        <days>15</days>
      </li>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_Housed">
        <share>0.75</share>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Medieval</defName>
    <label>medieval</label>
    <description>Iron and steel, castles and cathedrals, and the long road toward gunpowder.</description>
    <order>4</order>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.5</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>18</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>2.5</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_Population">
        <count>24</count>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Industrial</defName>
    <label>industrial</label>
    <description>Steam, steel mills, and the factory line remake how a civilization builds.</description>
    <order>5</order>
    <techLevel>Industrial</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>0.78</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>12</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>3</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_Population">
        <count>28</count>
      </li>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_FoodStores">
        <days>20</days>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Information</defName>
    <label>information</label>
    <description>Electronics, computing, and orbit turn a civilization's knowledge outward.</description>
    <order>6</order>
    <techLevel>Spacer</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.0</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>8</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>3.5</intendedYears>
    <conditions>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Research.EraCondition_Population">
        <count>32</count>
      </li>
    </conditions>
  </EraDef>

  <EraDef>
    <defName>Exotic</defName>
    <label>exotic</label>
    <description>Beyond the authored tree: the far end of the ladder, where SimWorld's later procedural extension picks up.</description>
    <order>7</order>
    <techLevel>Archotech</techLevel>
    <researchCostFactor>1.15</researchCostFactor>
    <citizensPerScholar>6</citizensPerScholar>
    <intendedYears>4</intendedYears>
  </EraDef>

</Defs>
